unt', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nA: 1, Q: 1, 9: 2, 8: 1, 7: 1, 5: 2, 3: 1\nValid Moves:\nNo valid moves available. You must PASS.\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**The Imminent Victory (and My Inability to Affect It)**\n\nOkay, here\'s the situation. I\'m playing Dou Dizhu, and it\'s looking pretty good. I\'m on the peasant team, partnered with the player to my right. The Landlord, our opponent, is to my left. My hand?  Just a 10, Jack, and King – all singles.  The current "play to beat" is a pair of Kings played by my teammate.\n\nNow, my initial thought was to see if I *could* contribute, but a quick look at my hand confirms I *can\'t*.  I don\'t have a pair, let alone one stronger than Kings.  So, I can\'t beat the play.\n\nBut here\'s where it gets interesting.  My teammate has only *one* card left!  They\'ve been cleverly using pairs – 10s, Jacks, and now Kings.  The Landlord passed on those Kings. This means, if I pass, the round ends. My teammate gets the advantage and plays their final card, which means we win!\n\nThis wasn\'t immediately obvious, but when I took a step back and looked at the game history (which I had to remind myself to do), it clicked. If I *could* beat it (which I can\'t, but let\'s hypothetically say I had a better pair), I *shouldn\'t*. Giving the Landlord an advantage would be bad play. I need to let my teammate win the round.\n\nThe prompt confirms what I already know: I have no valid moves. I *must* pass.  It\'s the only option, and it\'s the right option. The teammate is in the position to win, and it is a trivial step for my teammate to finish the game.\n\nSo, I\'m going to pass. It\'s the strategic move, and it\'s also the only move available to me. We\'re on our way to victory!\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: I hold a single 10, J, and K. The current play to beat is a Pair of Kings played by my teammate. I do not possess any pairs, nor any bombs to beat this hand. Furthermore, the provided list of valid moves indicates no moves are available. Even if I could play, my teammate has 1 card left and the landlord has passed; letting my teammate win the round allows them to play their final card and win the game for our team.\n\nReason: I physically cannot beat the Pair of Kings with my current hand of singles (10, J, K), and the system confirms there are no valid moves available. Passing is forced and also strategically correct to let the teammate finish the game.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
